{"chunk_id": "6870e4eaca31", "text": "After the Kurukshetra war, Yudhishthira and the Pandavas go to meet the grieving Dhritarashtra. Still burning with rage, the blind king attempts to crush Bhima in a deadly embrace, but Krishna secretly substitutes an iron statue, which Dhritarashtra shatters\u2014realizing too late the depth of his wrath and grief. Krishna rebukes him for ignoring wise counsel that could have prevented the war. Reconciliation follows, but new tensions arise when Gandhari, mourning her hundred sons, nearly curses the Pandavas. Guided by Vyasa, she restrains herself, though she reproaches Bhima for striking Duryodhana unfairly. The chapter culminates in a devastating vision of the battlefield: widows searching for bodies among the carnage. Gandhari\u2019s lament over Duryodhana and her sons transforms the victory into a portrait of irreversible loss and the tragic cost of ambition and vengeance.\nAfter the war, grieving Dhritarashtra tries to crush Bhima but is tricked; Gandhari confronts the Pandavas as women lament the devastation of Kurukshetra.\nThemes: aftermath of war, grief and lamentation, wrath and forgiveness, moral responsibility, divine intervention", "vector": [-0.009322556667029858, -0.015298554673790932, -0.010319694876670837, 0.003981722053140402, -0.0019840311724692583, 0.026321709156036377, -0.006071613635867834, 0.005019837990403175, -0.011453426443040371, -0.020707685500383377, 0.01644594594836235, 0.009732339531183243, 0.017470402643084526, 0.0130447493866086, -0.007300961762666702, 0.012737412936985493, 0.04286327213048935, 0.0005728420801460743, 0.04034993797540665, -0.029477035626769066, -0.0045246840454638, -0.0026226092595607042, -0.0004362478503026068, 0.013659423217177391, -0.01890464313328266, 0.020215947180986404, 0.011726615019142628, -0.019478337839245796, 0.013720891438424587, -0.011685636825859547, -0.02685442753136158, -0.005504747852683067, 0.014929750002920628, -0.008851306512951851, -0.01525757648050785, 0.010